\f1\b\fs24 \cf2 Installation:
\f0\b0 \cf0 \
\
Place the folder named "List Folder Files" in your iTunes Scripts folder. This is located in 
\f2\i \cf3 user name
\f0\i0  > Library > iTunes\cf0 . If there is no folder named "Scripts" there, create one and put the "List Folder Files" folder in it. This folder includes the "List Folder Files Not Added" AppleScript and the "ProgBar" application.\
\
(If you already have a copy of "ProgBar", then you can just place the script "List Folder Files Not Added" into your iTunes "Scripts" folder.)\
\
"ProgBar" is a small accessory application that provides visual feedback on the searching process. It's use is optional, as described below. "ProgBar" is written by T.J. Mahaffey of {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.sporksoftware.com/"}}{\fldrslt Spork Software}}. Many thanks! \
\

\f1\b \cf2 How to use:
\f0\b0 \cf0 \
\
You can run the script by selecting it from iTunes' Script menu. Or, you can double-click on its icon in the Finder.\
\
\'a5 You will be asked if you want to search your designated "Music" folder or to choose another folder containing audio files. The designated "Music" folder is the one set in iTunes' Advanced Preferences. Any other folder selected will have its complete contents searched for audio files, no matter how deep they may be. So, you 
\f2\i could
\f0\i0  select an entire drive.\
\
\'a5 You will be asked if you want Spotlight to re-index the selected folder. This will take a little extra time.\
\
\'a5 You will be asked if you want to use the "ProgBar" application to display the script's progress. Using "ProgBar" may make the script run longer. However, without it, you will not know how far along the script is proceeding.\
\
Once these options have been selected, the script will "walk" the selected folder looking for audio files and will check which are contained in the iTunes database. Those that are not will have their file path's added to a list which will be written to your desktop when the script is finished.\
\
